{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness, specifically defining a lottery as right-skewed when a greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes (i.e., the 0 Euro outcome) compared to favorable outcomes (i.e., the positive payout). In this decision problem, both lotteries have a non-zero probability of yielding 0 Euros (67% for B, 66% for A), and a positive payout (2500 for B, 2400 for A). The unfavorable outcome (0 Euros) dominates the favorable outcome in both cases. To assess skewness, we compare the probability mass on the unfavorable (0 Euro) outcome. Lottery B has a 67% chance of 0 Euros, while Lottery A has 66%. Therefore, Lottery B has a greater probability assigned to the unfavorable outcome, making it more right-skewed. The decision reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery, which in this case is Lottery B. Thus, the decision reason is applicable and can be evaluated using the given outcomes and probabilities.",
  "step_2": "The verbal report states: 'mehr Geld bei quasi gleichen Gewinnchancen' which translates to 'more money with almost identical winning chances'. This indicates the individual focused on the amount of money (expected value or payoff) and perceived the winning probabilities as very similar. The report does not mention anything about the distribution of outcomes, probabilities of zero, skewness, or unfavorable outcomes. The individual appears to be emphasizing the higher monetary amount (2500 vs 2400) and the similarity in probabilities, suggesting a focus on expected value or direct monetary gain rather than on the shape of the distribution (e.g., skewness). There is no reference to the probability of receiving zero, nor to the asymmetry of outcomes. Thus, the individual's consideration is centered on monetary amount and probability similarity, not on skewness or the imbalance between favorable and unfavorable outcomes.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating skewness—specifically, the proportion of probability assigned to unfavorable (0 Euro) outcomes—and preferring the more right-skewed lottery (i.e., higher probability of 0 Euros). In this case, Lottery B has a 67% chance of 0 Euros (vs 66% for A), so it is more right-skewed. However, the verbal report does not mention skewness, probability distribution, or the unfavorable outcomes. Instead, it focuses on the higher monetary amount and similar chances of winning. Therefore, the individual did not consider the skewness of the lotteries or apply the rule of preferring the more right-skewed lottery. The justification in the report is inconsistent with the decision reason. Hence, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
